1
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:09,980
Hi, I'm Paul Roussel, Vice President at TSMC North America.

2
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:14,756
TSMC is the company that manufactures the world's most advanced chips.

3
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:20,740
The ones that go into your phone, the ones that answer your questions on AI, the ones that might self-drive your car.

4
00:00:21,134 --> 00:00:31,940
I have a PhD in Applied Physics, and I've spent 25 years at TSMC watching how the things we do get smaller and smaller, while what they accomplish gets bigger and bigger.

5
00:00:32,686 --> 00:00:41,060
Today, I'm going to explain how a chip works, first to a kid, then to a college student, then to someone who does this for a living.

6
00:00:41,678 --> 00:00:43,800
Let's start small, fittingly.

7
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:49,410
Good morning, Elena.

8
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:50,210
How are you doing today?

9
00:00:50,590 --> 00:00:50,850
Good.

10
00:00:51,070 --> 00:00:51,550
Yeah?

11
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:52,850
How old are you?

12
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:54,810
I'm eight, and I just turned eight.

13
00:00:54,990 --> 00:00:55,890
You just turned eight?

14
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:56,676
Happy birthday.

15
00:00:57,294 --> 00:00:57,530
Thank you.

16
00:00:57,870 --> 00:00:58,730
So you know what, Elena?

17
00:00:58,926 --> 00:01:01,570
I have a little present for you today.

18
00:01:02,286 --> 00:01:04,010
It's a postage stamp.

19
00:01:04,046 --> 00:01:08,010
But before I give it to you, you have to answer a riddle.

20
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:09,604
Okay, I love riddles.

21
00:01:11,214 --> 00:01:15,350
What's smaller than a postage stamp but is the smartest thing people have ever built?

22
00:01:15,630 --> 00:01:16,450
Uh, a robot?

23
00:01:16,910 --> 00:01:17,750
A robot?

24
00:01:17,902 --> 00:01:19,690
A robot could be very smart.

25
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:21,310
Bigger than a stamp, though, right?

26
00:01:22,030 --> 00:01:22,770
Oh, yeah.

27
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:25,170
Elena, what did you play with this morning?

28
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:26,628
I played with my tablet.

29
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:28,090
You played with your tablet?

30
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:32,510
So you were this close to touching the smartest thing people have ever built.

31
00:01:32,782 --> 00:01:34,090
Let me show you something Elena.

32
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:40,930
This is a board and this is a type of board you might find in your tablet And there's a bunch of chips on here.

33
00:01:41,198 --> 00:01:42,510
So is it this one?

34
00:01:42,710 --> 00:01:49,130
Uh-huh The crazy part is A chip looks a lot like a city.

35
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:50,630
Have you ever been to a big city?

36
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:52,110
Yeah, like this one.

37
00:01:52,334 --> 00:01:55,530
I'll show you a model This is a city.

38
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:57,310
Do you see it has streets?

39
00:01:57,310 --> 00:01:59,370
Oh, yeah, just like this one.

40
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:00,170
And buildings.

41
00:02:00,622 --> 00:02:05,910
And the building has libraries which store all of the information the chip needs to know.

42
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:07,530
Have you been to the library before?

43
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:09,090
Yeah, we pick up cool books.

44
00:02:09,166 --> 00:02:10,430
You pick up cool books, right?

45
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:12,670
Libraries is where all the information is stored.

46
00:02:12,878 --> 00:02:14,370
So the chip has its libraries.

47
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:18,244
It also has its buildings where it does its computations.

48
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:22,130
It does all the math. And then it has messengers.

49
00:02:22,318 --> 00:02:27,150
The cars are messenger cars, they're little zaps of electricity that go around carrying the messages.

50
00:02:27,662 --> 00:02:31,010
And the streets are like the teeny, teeny wires in the chip.

51
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:33,150
They're smaller than a human hair.

52
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:34,490
Wow.

53
00:02:35,278 --> 00:02:40,370
And so when you hit the screen on your tablet, it sends a message.

54
00:02:40,686 --> 00:02:44,452
And what would happen, Elena, if this city didn't have any traffic lights?

55
00:02:44,910 --> 00:02:53,590
Oh, um, I think that everything will keep on going, and then we'll crash, and then it'll be a little crazy.

56
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:53,950
Yes.

57
00:02:54,110 --> 00:02:54,630
It'll be a mess.

58
00:02:55,230 --> 00:02:58,276
So this city has billions of traffic lights.

59
00:02:58,630 --> 00:03:08,292
And what they do is they control the messenger car so they go exactly from the right building to the other building, carrying the right messages and doing all the work.

60
00:03:08,686 --> 00:03:09,790
OK, wow.

61
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:13,110
And that happens billions of times per second.

62
00:03:13,678 --> 00:03:17,130
So it's going from here to there, so it's very, very busy.

63
00:03:17,358 --> 00:03:18,530
Very, very busy, yeah.

64
00:03:18,766 --> 00:03:20,370
And that's how a chip works, Helena.

65
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:21,350
Wow.

66
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:23,770
So remember our board, Elena?

67
00:03:24,142 --> 00:03:24,510
Yeah.

68
00:03:24,654 --> 00:03:25,890
Take another look at it.

69
00:03:26,190 --> 00:03:27,750
Does it look like a city?

70
00:03:28,046 --> 00:03:30,430
It does, but very, very small.

71
00:03:30,798 --> 00:03:31,830
Very, very small.

72
00:03:32,206 --> 00:03:35,430
I can see the streets and they're smaller than a piece of hair.

73
00:03:35,598 --> 00:03:35,990
Right.

74
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:41,252
Now imagine we take this whole board and we shrink it to something like this.

75
00:03:42,286 --> 00:03:43,170
That must be hard.

76
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:46,150
This is already tiny, tiny, tiny.

77
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:47,410
Really, really small.

78
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:49,270
It's really, really small.

79
00:03:49,270 --> 00:03:54,850
So, have you ever dreamed, Elena, of creating a machine, building a machine?

80
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:55,130
Yes.

81
00:03:56,110 --> 00:03:57,930
What kind of machine would you build?

82
00:03:58,410 --> 00:03:59,510
A robot.

83
00:03:59,710 --> 00:04:00,370
A robot?

84
00:04:00,718 --> 00:04:01,490
What kind of robot?

85
00:04:02,318 --> 00:04:11,570
A robot that is pink, and it can clean my room, make me breakfast, and, you know, sort my socks.

86
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:14,270
So, I want you to close your eyes for a second, Elena.

87
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:14,850
Okay.

88
00:04:15,374 --> 00:04:17,290
You can't see anything now, right?

89
00:04:17,329 --> 00:04:17,769
No.

90
00:04:17,769 --> 00:04:19,970
You can still hear me, and you're thinking.

91
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:21,603
Where are you thinking?

92
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:24,050
In my brain.

93
00:04:24,150 --> 00:04:25,570
So what does this robot need?

94
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:27,230
A brain.

95
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:28,210
And what's that called?

96
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:29,050
A chip.

97
00:04:29,510 --> 00:04:30,030
Exactly.

98
00:04:30,478 --> 00:04:31,364
You got it.

99
00:04:31,590 --> 00:04:33,750
Well, Elena, you solved my riddle.

100
00:04:33,934 --> 00:04:34,570
I did?

101
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:35,370
You did.

102
00:04:35,550 --> 00:04:36,430
That was fun.

103
00:04:36,846 --> 00:04:37,330
Good.

104
00:04:37,450 --> 00:04:38,970
You get to keep the stamp.

105
00:04:39,054 --> 00:04:39,930
Oh, thank you.

106
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:41,770
And the chip.

107
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:42,630
Wow.

108
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:45,604
That way you can ask other people the same riddle.

109
00:04:46,414 --> 00:04:55,460
Well, first things first, I'm going to tell them that it's a super chip for like the electronics, not the chip that they eat.

110
00:04:55,918 --> 00:04:58,010
Yeah, you want to make sure they don't eat that chip.

111
00:04:58,150 --> 00:04:58,730
Oh, yeah.

112
00:04:58,870 --> 00:05:00,970
But then you can teach them what a real chip is.

113
00:05:01,490 --> 00:05:01,890
Yeah.

114
00:05:02,414 --> 00:05:04,210
It was a pleasure talking to you, Elena.

115
00:05:04,398 --> 00:05:05,250
Thank you so much.

116
00:05:05,410 --> 00:05:05,828
You're welcome.

117
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:12,780
Hey, Joe, great to meet you.

118
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:13,780
Nice to meet you too, Paul.

119
00:05:14,574 --> 00:05:17,280
So, I hear you're a student in electrical engineering.

120
00:05:17,486 --> 00:05:19,020
Tell me a little bit about what you've been studying.

121
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:19,620
I

122
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:25,892
study analog integrated circuits with a focus on semiconductors and MOSFETs, specifically.

123
00:05:26,510 --> 00:05:34,720
So, Joe, I'm curious, tell me a little bit what you know about scaling, how we've been on this trajectory of scaling transistors and semiconductors for many years.

124
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:44,300
I know about Moore's Law, the idea that every two years, I think, we basically can double the amount of transistors that we can fit into a given area.

125
00:05:44,654 --> 00:05:45,960
So Joe, let's demonstrate.

126
00:05:45,960 --> 00:05:50,420
I'm going to give you a piece of paper, and I want you to fold it in half.

127
00:05:57,294 --> 00:05:57,820
There you go.

128
00:05:58,020 --> 00:05:59,680
That's one generation of Moore's Law.

129
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,240
That wasn't that hard, was it?

130
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:02,220
Yeah, it was pretty easy.

131
00:06:04,110 --> 00:06:06,580
Except we've been doing this for over 30 generations.

132
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:09,240
So keep folding, Joe.

133
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:13,120
I don't think I'm going to be able to fold this 30 times, but I'll give it a shot.

134
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,940
Think about the smartphone I have.

135
00:06:16,494 --> 00:06:20,548
It has the power of a supercomputer from the mid-90s.

136
00:06:21,006 --> 00:06:25,160
And not only that, that supercomputer would have cost me $50 million.

137
00:06:25,966 --> 00:06:28,132
I can get this for about $1,000.

138
00:06:29,102 --> 00:06:30,740
That's the power of Moore's Law.

139
00:06:31,054 --> 00:06:31,440
That's amazing.

140
00:06:31,886 --> 00:06:32,548
It is amazing.

141
00:06:33,326 --> 00:06:34,120
Joe, you're slowing down.

142
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:34,700
What's going on?

143
00:06:35,534 --> 00:06:39,396
So, I'm running out of the strength and the...

144
00:06:39,822 --> 00:06:40,720
I mean, it's getting too thick.

145
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:41,840
It's getting thick.

146
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,840
As you keep scaling, it gets harder and harder.

147
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:51,000
For example, the name of our latest technology in production is 2 nanometers.

148
00:06:51,438 --> 00:06:54,148
That's basically 10 atoms across.

149
00:06:55,086 --> 00:06:55,340
Wow.

150
00:06:55,886 --> 00:07:00,644
I want to show you a wafer that we built right here in the US.

151
00:07:01,262 --> 00:07:06,920
This is a 300 millimeter wafer, and it's on 4 nanometers.

152
00:07:07,566 --> 00:07:12,080
And if you look at it carefully, you can see the individual chips in there.

153
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:16,060
So what do you think about the thickness versus the size of this thing?

154
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:18,380
Well, you said it's 300 millimeters in diameter.

155
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:21,260
Yeah, it looks to me about one millimeter thick, maybe.

156
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:21,780
Right.

157
00:07:21,966 --> 00:07:25,260
That's the whole point is a lot of silicon is two-dimensional.

158
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,840
The actual thickness we use for circuits is actually less than one.

159
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:30,920
That one millimeter is there for structure.

160
00:07:31,340 --> 00:07:31,500
Right.

161
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:35,204
In a real circuit, it's actually thinner than this piece of paper.

162
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:42,920
I'm gonna fold this and think about if I had a big chip before, or what happens when I stack it, right?

163
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:49,380
So I was trying to communicate from this end of the paper to this end of the paper, it's pretty long distance.

164
00:07:50,478 --> 00:07:52,340
What happens now when I fold it together?

165
00:07:52,782 --> 00:07:56,420
The distance of that communication, that interconnect is much smaller.

166
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:00,300
And so that means it's much faster and it's much more power efficient.

167
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:00,880
Right.

168
00:08:01,294 --> 00:08:09,580
That's another way to keep scaling the compute of our system by just adding more and more layers in the third dimension.

169
00:08:10,140 --> 00:08:10,600
That's amazing.

170
00:08:10,926 --> 00:08:15,660
So when you think about everything we talked about, Joe, what excites you the most about the future?

171
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:22,760
Well, as an aspiring engineer, I think the idea that we're really running up against the laws of physics is very exciting.

172
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:35,520
Finding ways around kind of the universe that we live in order to make better machines that can enhance our lives is very interesting and I'm really excited to be entering this field.

173
00:08:35,662 --> 00:08:39,320
Really happy to have you join the club because we need a lot of smart people like you.

174
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:39,980
Thank you, Joe.

175
00:08:40,206 --> 00:08:40,659
Thank you, Paul.

176
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:47,400
Welcome, Stephanie.

177
00:08:47,520 --> 00:08:48,220
How are you doing today?

178
00:08:48,462 --> 00:08:49,300
I'm doing well, and you?

179
00:08:49,582 --> 00:08:50,340
Good, thank you.

180
00:08:50,894 --> 00:08:52,860
So you're a professor at Penn State.

181
00:08:53,180 --> 00:08:55,140
Can you describe what you do there?

182
00:08:55,278 --> 00:09:01,660
Yeah, so I'm a professor of material science and engineering, and we make new materials, atomic layer by atomic layer.

183
00:09:01,966 --> 00:09:04,820
I know you do a lot of your work on 2D materials.

184
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:06,840
That's continuing the scaling.

185
00:09:07,150 --> 00:09:08,700
Can you talk a little bit about that?

186
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:09,500
Absolutely.

187
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:11,800
Right now, your transistors are what?

188
00:09:12,334 --> 00:09:14,300
10 atoms thick of that order.

189
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:20,460
It turns out for a material like silicon, there's a limit to how small you can make a silicon transistor.

190
00:09:21,166 --> 00:09:28,180
And if we want to keep packing more and more transistors on a chip to make our chips more and more powerful, we need to look at new materials.

191
00:09:28,460 --> 00:09:32,440
And one material class that we can think about are 2D materials.

192
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:37,020
These are materials that are literally one or two layers of atoms thick.

193
00:09:37,262 --> 00:09:48,320
So you can imagine if you go from a transistor made of 10 atoms to a transistor made of only one atom, now maybe you can pack 10 times as many transistors on your chip and now your chip can be 10 times as powerful.

194
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:53,040
So there's a lot of, you know, great stuff that's happening on new material research.

195
00:09:53,358 --> 00:09:54,240
But what about packaging?

196
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:57,820
How do you see things shifting in packaging these days?

197
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:00,100
Packaging is an interesting topic.

198
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:08,520
If you're able to, instead of making one big chip that has to compute here in the memory and whatever, if you can make all these different little chiplets.

199
00:10:09,230 --> 00:10:12,540
You can stack them up, you can package them inside one package.

200
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:20,600
Now, in addition to being able to go in the third dimension, you can also design each chiplet to do its own job as efficiently as possible.

201
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:23,748
Yeah, I'm so glad you mentioned that because here's an example.

202
00:10:24,430 --> 00:10:26,940
Here's one of our COASL's latest technology.

203
00:10:27,470 --> 00:10:37,620
So I have my main silicon, my HBM on the sides, and then on the top and the bottom I have my IO chips that communicate with the outside world.

204
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:42,660
So now I can have each piece of silicon optimized exactly for what it needs to do.

205
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:45,956
And that also means that you reduce your energy usage.

206
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:49,920
And on the consumer side, it also means that my cell phone battery lasts much longer.

207
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:51,200
Yes, exactly.

208
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:52,380
You can do so much more.

209
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:52,940
Exactly.

210
00:10:53,230 --> 00:10:56,840
Going back to Penn State with your students, what are you going to be working on now?

211
00:10:57,678 --> 00:10:59,040
What are we going to be working on?

212
00:10:59,310 --> 00:11:02,600
I mean, we're going to keep designing new materials for the future.

213
00:11:02,760 --> 00:11:05,700
We're going to keep working on 2D materials, trying to understand.

214
00:11:05,806 --> 00:11:10,120
One of the major problems right now in 2D materials is actually, it's called the doping problem.

215
00:11:10,606 --> 00:11:17,360
So how can we, in one material, change its properties just by adding tiny amounts of dopant materials?

216
00:11:17,806 --> 00:11:23,700
So this is actually a real challenge in 2D materials, and it's a challenge we need to solve if we want to use these materials for advanced transistors.

217
00:11:24,046 --> 00:11:25,640
It sounds like high-tech cooking, right?

218
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:27,860
How do you have just the right amount of spice to make a cake?

219
00:11:27,860 --> 00:11:29,360
It exactly is like that, right?

220
00:11:29,454 --> 00:11:32,900
So when you want to, you know, say you want to make chocolate chip cookies.

221
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:33,880
What do you do?

222
00:11:34,062 --> 00:11:37,828
You go to the store, you buy the bag of chocolate chips, you follow the recipe on the back.

223
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:40,180
We can do the same thing with material science.

224
00:11:40,366 --> 00:11:46,460
If we want to make a known material, we can look in literature, find its recipe, follow the recipe, get the material.

225
00:11:46,830 --> 00:11:49,740
But if you want to bake something new, what do you do?

226
00:11:50,190 --> 00:11:51,880
Well, you try some things.

227
00:11:51,980 --> 00:11:55,300
Maybe you start from a recipe you know, you change a thing here, you change it there.

228
00:11:55,360 --> 00:12:00,800
And eventually, maybe you have a new, better recipe for chocolate chip cookies or a totally different kind of cookie.

229
00:12:01,230 --> 00:12:02,820
And that's exactly what we're doing, right?

230
00:12:02,820 --> 00:12:04,000
We start from a known recipe.

231
00:12:04,590 --> 00:12:08,200
We change things a little bit here and there and see if we can't make our materials even better.

232
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:11,380
So what excites you most about where we're headed?

233
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:22,420
You know, I think the thing I'm most excited about is actually the ability to do this really interdisciplinary work where people who have different expertise work together to try to solve a big problem.

234
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:25,956
And this is the only way that we're gonna be able to solve the big problems that we have nowadays.

235
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:28,140
Yeah, yeah, beautifully said.

236
00:12:28,526 --> 00:12:29,580
We have a name for that.

237
00:12:29,934 --> 00:12:40,420
we call it STCO, System Technology Co-Optimization, which basically means that people can't go off and design everything in their own sandbox.

238
00:12:40,700 --> 00:12:41,780
They have to work together.

239
00:12:42,020 --> 00:12:50,120
I think one of the things that I'm really excited about is the ability to use AI and machine learning to accelerate materials development.

240
00:12:50,620 --> 00:13:01,900
Right now, we get so much data from our experiments, and I'm sure that you have orders of magnitude more data than we do, And trying to spot that needle in the haystack for a human is very difficult.

241
00:13:02,190 --> 00:13:04,320
It's too much data for one person to think about.

242
00:13:04,814 --> 00:13:09,340
But artificial intelligence, it doesn't have those same limitations of looking at huge data sets.

243
00:13:09,390 --> 00:13:15,200
So it can look at all the data and find the signal that we're really interested in to solve some of these problems.

244
00:13:15,950 --> 00:13:16,820
Well, thank you, Stephanie.

245
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:18,820
It's been a real pleasure talking to you.

246
00:13:19,182 --> 00:13:24,420
And I want to wish you and your students a lot of luck and hope you find many needles in those haystacks.

247
00:13:24,580 --> 00:13:25,444
Thank you so much.

248
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:26,420
I

249
00:13:26,420 --> 00:13:33,340
hope you enjoyed learning about how some of the teeniest technology in the world is powering the biggest advances in artificial intelligence.

250
00:13:34,318 --> 00:13:41,800
It's a great time to be an engineer, or to become one, because there's plenty of tough problems to solve, and we need to solve them together.

251
00:13:42,894 --> 00:13:48,960
It's also a great time to be alive, because with AI, nothing has to be done the way it was done before.

252
00:13:48,960 --> 00:13:58,440
All you need is an idea and an AI platform running on chips designed by our customers that we built with atomic precision.

253
00:13:58,958 --> 00:14:00,060
And here's the part I love.

254
00:14:00,942 --> 00:14:02,948
With AI, there's a virtuous cycle at work.

255
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:09,160
AI is now helping us design the next generation of chips, which will power the next generation of AI.

256
00:14:09,966 --> 00:14:22,080
So somewhere out there, there's a chip we haven't built yet, doing things we haven't imagined yet, may be designed by a kid who's holding a postage stamp right now.

